insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 1, 'Every other course in this ladder describes rock as it stands today. What makes a basin model different?', '["It maps seismic reflectors to a higher standard of accuracy than an interpreter can.","It runs the clock, reconstructing where each layer sat and how hot it was through time, so that events can be dated and put in order.","It converts a mapped structure into a barrel count.","It measures porosity in a borehole."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A basin model is the only workflow in the ladder that runs the clock rather than describing a snapshot. Mapping, volumetrics and log analysis all describe the present day, and none of them can date an event.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 2, 'Which of these is one of the three questions a basin model answers?', '["What mud weight will keep the hole stable through the reservoir section?","How many barrels sit in the mapped structure?","Did this basin ever generate hydrocarbons, and when?","What is the grain density of the reservoir?"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The three questions are whether the basin ever generated, when it generated, and whether the charge reached anywhere. Barrel counts belong to volumetrics and mud weight to the pore pressure workflow.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 3, 'A shale bed is 100 m thick today with its top at 1000 m. Restore the same grain to the surface and it occupies 159.79553483785466 m. What does that say about reading past depths off today''s section?', '["Nothing, because the layer has always been 100 m thick and only its depth below the surface has changed since it was deposited.","Past depth can be recovered by subtracting whatever has been deposited since that time.","Every layer was thicker in the past, so past depths have to be reconstructed.","The layer must have been thinner in the past."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The layer has lost 59.79553483785466 m of thickness to compaction, so everything beneath it once sat deeper than a simple subtraction suggests. Treating today''s thickness as constant, or subtracting only later deposition, is the classic error.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 4, 'What does the timing element of a petroleum system require?', '["That the seal has a capillary entry pressure high enough to hold a hydrocarbon column.","That the source rock contains enough organic carbon of a suitable kerogen type.","That migration is driven by buoyancy along a carrier bed.","That the trap and seal exist before the charge arrives."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Timing is the requirement that the five physical elements line up on one clock, with trap and seal in place before the charge arrives. The other three options describe source, seal and migration, which are separate elements.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 5, 'Which element of a petroleum system can fail even when every physical ingredient is present and correct?', '["Seal, because every seal leaks at some rate however fine grained it is.","Timing, since a source that matured and expelled before the trap and seal existed leaves a perfect structure with nothing in it.","Source, because organic carbon is consumed during burial.","Trap, because closure is always uncertain."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Timing has no physical existence of its own, so it is the element that can fail with a good source, a good trap and a good seal all present. That is why the workflow that dates events is the one that can test it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 6, 'What is primary migration, also called expulsion?', '["The journey of hydrocarbons up through carrier beds and along faults, driven by buoyancy.","The filling of a trap to its spill point.","The departure of generated fluid from the source rock itself.","The conversion of kerogen into oil."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Expulsion is the first stage, the fluid leaving the source rock, and it does not happen until enough has been generated to push out of the source''s own pore space. The journey afterwards is secondary migration, which is the usual confusion.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 7, 'In what sense is a seal effective?', '["A seal is effective when it leaks more slowly than the trap fills.","A seal is effective when it is literally impermeable to hydrocarbons at every scale of measurement.","A seal is effective when it sits directly above a mature source rock.","A seal is effective when it is thicker than the reservoir."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Seal quality is a question of capillary entry pressure rather than of a rock being impermeable, and every seal leaks eventually. A working seal is one that leaks more slowly than the trap is charged.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 8, 'A shale carries abundant organic carbon of a good kerogen type but never reached the temperatures required. How is it described?', '["As a seal failure, because the hydrocarbons escaped through the overlying section before the trap formed.","As a trap failure, because there was no closure to receive the charge.","As a migration failure, since the charge never left the source rock.","As a source rock that is present and unreacted."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A source rock is a claim about potential, and maturity is how far that potential was actually taken. A rock that never got hot enough is present and immature, which is a maturity failure rather than a migration, trap or seal failure.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 9, 'Which two ingredients does this tier build, and why those two?', '["Maturity and migration, because they decide whether a discovery exists.","Trap and seal, because those are the elements you can map.","Source and timing, because they come first in the sequence.","Burial and heat, because both are geometry and steady conduction, can be checked by hand, and stand on their own."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Burial and heat are closed form and checkable, and every later stage takes the temperature history they produce as an input. The split is a matter of dependency rather than of difficulty.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 10, 'Where do maturity kinetics sit in this ladder?', '["In the Expert tier, alongside generation, expulsion and the signature of a 600 m erosion event.","In this tier, immediately after the heat column.","In the Professional tier, taking the temperature history built at this tier as its input.","They are not covered anywhere in the ladder."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Professional tier runs the maturity kinetics on the temperature history this tier produces, and the Expert tier runs the full forward model with generation, expulsion and the erosion signature. Neither is examined here.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 11, 'The engine and an independently written Python implementation of the same physics agree to about 1e-9 on the kinetics and about 1e-4 m on the geometry. What does that agreement establish?', '["That both programs evaluate the written equations correctly.","That the compaction and conduction laws used are a correct description of any real sedimentary basin.","That the fitted parameters in the lithology library are the right values for every basin.","That the fixture was measured in a laboratory."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Two people writing the same equations in two languages make different mistakes, so agreement in the ninth digit is strong evidence that both are computing what was written down. It is not evidence that the equations or the parameters describe a particular field.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 12, 'A thickness in this course is graded to a tolerance of 0.05 m, while the two implementations differ by about 1e-4 m on the geometry. What follows?', '["An answer outside tolerance is your arithmetic, not the fixture.","The tolerance should be tightened to 1e-4 m so that grading matches the agreement between the implementations.","An answer that misses the tolerance is evidence that the fixture itself carries an error.","Answers must be reported to seventeen digits to stay inside the tolerance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tolerance sits orders of magnitude above the disagreement between the two implementations, so numerical noise cannot push an answer across it. Any miss is arithmetic.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 13, 'In the golden heat fixture, what is the basal heat flow?', '["60 W/m2, since heat flow is quoted in whole watts.","0.06 W/m2, which is the same quantity written as 60 mW/m2.","0.06 mW/m2 at the base of the column.","6 W/m2 at the base."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture drives 0.06 W/m2 through the column, and 60 mW/m2 is the same number in the units the literature usually uses. Dropping or adding the milli prefix is the standard slip with heat flow.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 14, 'How is the golden heat column laid out?', '["Two layers of 1000 m each, the upper with a conductivity of 3.5 W/m/K over a lower layer of 1.8 W/m/K, divided into ten cells of 100 m each.","Two layers of 1000 m each, the upper with a conductivity of 1.8 W/m/K over a lower layer of 3.5 W/m/K, in ten cells of 100 m each.","One uniform layer of 2000 m with a conductivity of 1.8 W/m/K.","Two layers of 100 m with cell centres every 10 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fixture puts the lower conductivity of 1.8 W/m/K on top of the higher conductivity of 3.5 W/m/K, ten cells of 100 m in each layer, so the cell centres run from 50 m to 1950 m. Swapping the two conductivities is the easy mistake.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-why-basin-modelling', 15, 'Which value is the graded shale porosity at 2000 m?', '["0.22717481230903933 v/v.","0.22717481230903933 percent, since porosity is always reported as a percentage.","0.63 v/v, which is the value the engine''s library holds for shale.","2.2717481230903933 v/v."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The graded value is a fraction of bulk volume, 0.22717481230903933 v/v at 2000 m, to a tolerance of 0.001. Reading the fraction as a percentage, or quoting the surface value of 0.63 v/v with no depth attached, are the two errors this question is built around.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 1, 'Why does the Sclater-Christie law make porosity fall exponentially rather than linearly with depth?', '["Because each load increment closes a share of the pore space that remains.","Because the grains themselves compress steadily under load, so every metre of burial removes the same volume of solid from the rock.","Because porosity is conventionally plotted against depth on a logarithmic axis.","Because compaction stops once the rock reaches a fixed depth."]'::jsonb, 0, 'What compacts is the pore space, and each load increment removes a share of what is left, so the loss rate is proportional to the porosity remaining. The option about grains compressing steadily has the mechanism backwards, since grains are close to incompressible at these stresses.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 2, 'Take the drop in shale porosity over the first 1000 m and repeat it downward as a straight line. What does that predict, and what does the engine return at 3000 m?', '["A porosity at 3000 m higher than the exponential gives, because a straight line falls more slowly than a curve at every depth.","Exactly the same answer as the exponential, since both pass through 0.63 v/v at the surface.","Zero porosity before 3000 m, where the engine returns 0.13641747040908445 v/v.","A negative porosity at 1000 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A linear law has a depth at which the rock ceases to exist, and repeating the first kilometre''s drop reaches zero before 3000 m. The engine still holds 0.13641747040908445 v/v there, which is why the exponential form is used.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 3, 'Which mechanism dominates porosity loss in the shallow section, and which takes over deeper?', '["Pressure solution first, because stress at grain contacts is highest immediately after deposition, then mechanical rearrangement deeper.","Cementation throughout, with no mechanical stage at all.","Mechanical rearrangement and dewatering first, then pressure solution once the section is hot enough.","Only mechanical rearrangement, at every depth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Grains rearrange and water is squeezed out as soon as load arrives, while pressure solution needs elevated temperature to run at any speed and therefore takes over with depth. Reversing the order is the common misreading.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 4, 'Why does a basin model track porosity rather than bulk density?', '["Because porosity is the space being destroyed, it is bounded and dimensionless, and grain density is fixed per lithology at values such as 2720 kg/m3 for shale.","Because density varies too much between basins to be useful in any model.","Because porosity and density are unrelated quantities in a sediment.","Because bulk density cannot be measured in a well."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Porosity is the variable the compaction law is written in, it has an obvious valid range, and since grain density is a constant of the lithology the bulk density can always be recovered from it. Nothing is lost by tracking the porosity.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 5, 'In $\phi(z) = \phi_0 e^{-cz}$, what is $\phi_0$?', '["The porosity at the base of the layer, which is why it is written with a subscript for the first depth of interest.","The rate at which pore space is lost per metre of burial.","The porosity of the sediment at the moment it is deposited, which is the value the curve returns at $z = 0$ whatever the compaction constant happens to be.","The compaction constant, in per m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Setting $z = 0$ makes the exponential equal to 1, so the curve returns $\phi_0$ at the surface for any compaction constant. The options describing a rate per metre are describing $c$, which is the parameter most often confused with $\phi_0$.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 6, 'What are the units of the compaction constant $c$, and what does it control?', '["It is dimensionless, and it controls the porosity the sediment has at the moment it is deposited.","In v/v, and it sets the surface porosity of the lithology in question.","In m, and it gives the depth at which porosity reaches zero.","Per m, and it controls how fast porosity falls with depth."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The product $cz$ in the exponent has to be dimensionless, so $c$ carries units of per m, and it sets the rate at which pore space is lost. Describing it as a surface porosity is the standard confusion with $\phi_0$.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 7, 'Hold $c$ fixed and lower $\phi_0$. What happens to the curve?', '["The curve bends down more steeply, so shallow porosities fall a great deal while the deep values are almost unchanged.","Every porosity scales down by the same proportion.","Only the porosity at the surface changes, and the deeper part of the curve is untouched.","The depth at which porosity would reach zero moves upward toward the surface."]'::jsonb, 1, '$\phi_0$ multiplies the exponential rather than sitting inside it, so it scales every depth alike and leaves the shape of the curve alone. Bending the curve is what $c$ does, and the first option is that confusion.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 8, 'What are the engine''s compaction parameters for shale?', '["$\\phi_0 = 0.49$ and $c = 0.00027$ per m, which is the pair the library holds for the finest grained lithology.","$\\phi_0 = 0.63$ percent and $c = 0.00051$ per km.","$\\phi_0 = 0.00051$ v/v and $c = 0.63$ per m.","$\\phi_0 = 0.63$ and $c = 0.00051$ per m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shale is deposited at 0.63 v/v and loses porosity at 0.00051 per m. The 0.49 and 0.00027 per m pair belongs to sandstone, and the remaining options swap the two parameters or change their units.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 9, 'Work shale porosity at 2000 m. What is the exponent, and what is the porosity?', '["The exponent is 1.02 and the porosity is 0.22717481230903933 v/v.","The exponent is 0.00102, since the depth should be entered in kilometres, and the porosity stays close to the surface value.","The exponent is 1.02 and the porosity is 22.717481230903933 v/v.","The exponent is 1.02 and the porosity is 0.63 v/v."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The exponent is $0.00051 \times 2000 = 1.02$, dimensionless, and scaling 0.63 by $e^{-1.02}$ gives 0.22717481230903933 v/v. Entering the depth in kilometres against a constant in per m is the classic units failure.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 10, 'A shale in another basin was deposited at a surface porosity of 0.50 but has the same compaction constant of 0.00051 per m. What share of its original pore space survives to 2000 m, compared with the engine''s shale?', '["A larger share, because a sediment that starts with less pore space has less to lose and therefore keeps proportionally more.","The same share, since the surviving fraction is $e^{-cz}$ and $\\phi_0$ cancels out of it.","It cannot be determined without the grain density.","A smaller share, in the ratio of 0.50 to 0.63."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Divide $\phi(z)$ by $\phi_0$ and the surface porosity disappears, leaving $e^{-cz}$, so the proportion surviving depends on the compaction constant alone. The other shale holds less pore space in absolute terms because it began with less.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 11, 'What is the solid thickness of 100 m of freshly deposited shale at the surface, and what does it represent?', '["A larger share, because a sediment that starts with less pore space has less to lose and therefore keeps proportionally more.","The same share, since the surviving fraction is $e^{-cz}$ and the surface porosity cancels out of that ratio entirely, leaving the compaction constant to decide it.","It cannot be determined without the grain density.","A smaller share, in the ratio of 0.50 to 0.63."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Solid thickness adds up the grain fraction over the layer, and for 100 m of fresh shale it is 38.57953418711555 m, so fresh shale is more pore than grain. Reading solid thickness as the layer thickness is the misconception this question targets.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 12, 'Which lithology holds the most grain in 100 m of freshly deposited sediment, and which parameter decides it?', '["Shale, at 38.57953418711555 m, because its compaction constant is the largest in the library at 0.00051 per m.","Sandstone, at 51.655586470092686 m, because a well sorted sand packs tightest when it first comes to rest.","Dolomite, at 50.98679894040397 m, because its grain density is 2600 kg/m3.","Limestone, at 55.77839233115688 m, from the lowest $\\phi_0$ in the library at 0.45."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Over an interval this thin and this shallow the compaction constant has almost no room to act, so the ranking follows the surface porosity, and limestone starts lowest at 0.45. Shale, starting highest at 0.63, holds the least grain.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 13, 'At 2000 m, which of shale and sandstone is the more porous, and why?', '["Sandstone, at 0.285547 v/v against 0.227175 v/v, because its compaction constant of 0.00027 per m is the smaller.","Shale, at 0.227175 v/v against 0.285547 v/v, because the rock with the higher surface porosity keeps that lead at every depth.","They are equal at 2000 m, which is where the two curves cross.","Sandstone, because its grain density is 2650 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Shale bends down at 0.00051 per m against sandstone''s 0.00027 per m, so it spends its surface lead faster and is behind by 2000 m. Assuming that the most porous rock at the surface stays the most porous at depth is the misconception being tested.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 14, 'Where does the porosity crossover between shale and sandstone sit?', '["Below 3000 m, since shale still holds 0.136417 v/v there against sandstone''s 0.217980 v/v, so the two have not yet crossed.","At the surface, where shale sits at 0.630000 v/v and sandstone at 0.490000 v/v, which is where the two curves meet.","Between 1000 m, where shale still leads at 0.378312 v/v against 0.374056 v/v, and 2000 m.","There is no crossover for this pair."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Shale is barely ahead at 1000 m and clearly behind at 2000 m, so the curves cross between those two depths. At 3000 m sandstone is already well ahead at 0.217980 v/v, which is after the crossing rather than before it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-compaction', 15, 'Compare two different 100 m intervals of the same shale, one freshly deposited at the surface and one with its top at 1000 m. How does the solid thickness of the deeper interval compare?', '["It is unchanged at 38.57953418711555 m, because solid thickness is conserved and cannot depend on where a layer is found.","It rises to 63.11728183077296 m, because porosity at 1000 m is far lower, so a larger share of every metre of that interval is grain.","It falls, because compaction removes grain as well as pore space.","It rises to 159.79553483785466 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Porosity at 1000 m is far below the surface value, so a 100 m interval down there contains 63.11728183077296 m of grain against 38.57953418711555 m at the surface. Conservation applies to one layer followed through time, not to two different 100 m intervals at different depths.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 1, 'A well report lists a shale interval as 100 m thick. What does that number represent in a basin model?', '["Its present-day thickness, which is what remains after compaction and is smaller than the thickness the layer had when it was deposited","Its thickness on the day the last grain of it was deposited","Its solid thickness, with the pore space removed","Its average thickness across the basin"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Logged thicknesses are present-day thicknesses. The depositional thickness is recovered by decompaction, and solid thickness is a separate quantity that takes the pore space out.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 2, 'A 100 m shale with its top at 1000 m is restored to the surface. Which quantity is unchanged by that restoration?', '["Its total thickness, because decompaction relocates a layer without adding or removing any material","Its average porosity, which is fixed by the lithology alone","Its solid thickness","Its porosity at its base"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Decompaction conserves grain and moves thickness around. Expecting thickness to be conserved is the classic beginner error, since thickness is the quantity that changes most.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 3, 'How much solid grain is contained in 100 m of freshly deposited shale at the surface?', '["63.11728183077296 m, which is the grain in 100 m of the same shale found at 1000 m","38.57953418711555 m","100 m, since grain and thickness are the same thing at the surface","159.79553483785466 m"]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the surface the shale starts at porosity 0.63, so 100 m of it holds 38.57953418711555 m of grain. The 63.11728183077296 m figure belongs to the same 100 m found at 1000 m, where the pores have closed.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 4, '100 m of shale with its top at 1000 m is restored to the surface. What thickness does it occupy there?', '["63.11728183077296 m, which is what the compaction integral returns for the layer in place","59.79553483785466 m, which is what the layer gained on restoration","134.010303 m","159.79553483785466 m"]'::jsonb, 3, '159.79553483785466 m is the graded restoration. 63.11728183077296 m is the grain inside the layer, 59.79553483785466 m is the thickness it lost, and 134.010303 m is the restoration of a layer found at 500 m.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 5, 'Restoring a buried layer to a shallower depth changes its thickness in which direction, and why?', '["It thickens, because the same grain sits further apart where porosity is higher","It thins, because removing the overburden lets the grains relax into a tighter packing arrangement","It stays the same, because no material was added","It thickens or thins depending on the lithology"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Restoration moves a fixed amount of grain up the porosity curve, where the curve says the grains sit further apart, so the layer grows. Expecting it to thin reverses the direction of compaction.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 6, 'A 100 m shale found at 3000 m restores to 214.973300 m while a 100 m shale found at 500 m restores to 134.010303 m. What explains the difference?', '["The deeper layer has had longer to compact, since compaction is driven by elapsed time rather than by depth","The deeper layer holds more grain in its present 100 m, so putting that grain back at surface porosity takes more room","The deeper layer has a lower grain density","The two layers were deposited at different rates"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Porosity falls with depth, so a 100 m interval at 3000 m is packed with grain and grows more on restoration. In this model the curve depends on depth rather than on elapsed time.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 7, 'The solid thickness of the restored 159.79553483785466 m of shale is recomputed at the surface as a check. What value confirms the restoration?', '["63.117281830772924 m","159.79553483785466 m, because the restored thickness is the quantity that was conserved","38.57953418711555 m","100 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The check returns 63.117281830772924 m against the 63.11728183077296 m that went in, agreeing to about 1e-13 m. Answering with the restored thickness confuses the conserved grain with the thickness it happens to occupy.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 8, 'A round trip through a decompaction step returns a grain thickness that differs from the input by several metres. What is the most likely cause?', '["Floating point noise in the solver, which grows with the number of iterations","A lithology or top depth mismatch between the restoration and the check","The layer was restored to the surface rather than to an intermediate depth","The porosity curve flattens at depth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Solver noise is of the order of 1e-13 m, so a mismatch of metres is a real error. Lithology mix-ups and mismatched top depths are the usual causes, along with an unconverged solver or a compaction constant given per kilometre instead of per metre.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 9, 'A decompaction run conserves grain to about 1e-13 m at every layer. Which conclusion does that support?', '["The compaction parameters used are the right ones for the rock in this well","The layer has never been buried more deeply in the past than it is today","The restored thicknesses can now be reported without any further checking of the inputs","Each restoration solved the equation it was given"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The invariant tests the calculation rather than the model. Wrong curve parameters and a deeper past burial both pass it untouched, which is why a passing check has to be reported with what it covers.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 10, 'A 100 m shale found at 1000 m restores to 159.795535 m and its compaction loss is quoted as 37.4200 percent. What is the denominator of that percentage?', '["The present-day thickness of 100 m that the well report gives","The solid thickness of 63.117282 m","The original restored thickness of 159.795535 m","The 1000 m depth at which the layer is found today"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Compaction loss is quoted against the original thickness. Setting the same loss against the present-day 100 m gives a much larger number for the same event, which is why the denominator has to be stated.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 11, 'Compaction loss against original thickness is 25.3789 percent for a layer found at 500 m and 48.5896 percent for one found at 2000 m. Why does the share rise with depth?', '["Because the deeper layer is thinner today, so the same loss is a larger share of it","Because the deeper layer sits further down the porosity curve, so more of its original pore volume has already been driven out","Because grain density increases with depth","Because the deeper layer is older"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Both layers are 100 m thick today, so the difference is not one of denominators. The deeper layer holds 77.852091 m of grain against 63.117282 m at 1000 m, which means more of its original pore space has gone.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 12, 'What does backstripping do?', '["It removes the youngest layer, decompacts everything that remains to its new shallower depths, and repeats down the stack","It restores every layer in the well to the surface at once","It converts a present-day temperature profile into a burial history by inverting the measured gradient","It fits a porosity curve to log data"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Backstripping is strip, decompact, record, repeat, and decompaction is the step inside it. Every remaining layer regrows at every step rather than merely rising.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 13, 'A burial history is built by stacking present-day thicknesses with no decompaction anywhere. How is it wrong?', '["Every layer is placed deeper in the past than it really was","Only the deepest layer is affected, and only by a small amount","The geometry is correct, and the error appears later when temperatures are computed from that geometry","Every layer is placed too shallow, and the early burial curve is also too flat"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Compacted thicknesses make the reconstructed column too thin, so every layer sits too shallow. Because compaction is fastest in the first kilometre, the error also varies through the history and flattens the early part of the curve.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 14, 'Which of these can decompaction on its own not recover?', '["The thickness a layer had when it was deposited","The depth of a layer at an earlier moment in the basin''s history","Section that has been eroded away","The average porosity a layer had at a shallower depth"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Porosity remembers the deepest burial, so eroded section has to be put back by hand before the restoration is right. Recovering an erosion event from its thermal signature is Expert tier work.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-decompaction', 15, 'Why does an error in decompaction propagate into everything else a basin model reports?', '["Because the basal heat flow is computed from the total thickness of the layers above it","Because past depth sets past temperature, which sets the timing","Because solid thickness is used as the cell spacing in the heat solver","Because porosity is used to calculate the surface temperature"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The model is a chain that starts with geometry. A wrong depth history gives a wrong temperature history, and no care taken further down the chain recovers from it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 1, 'The golden fixture uses a basal heat flow of 0.06 W/m2. How else is that value written?', '["0.06 mW/m2","6 mW/m2, a typical continental value","60 mW/m2","600 mW/m2, which would suit a young rift"]'::jsonb, 2, '0.06 W/m2 is 60 mW/m2. Heat flow is small in watts per square metre, which is why the field normally quotes it in milliwatts per square metre, and either form is fine as long as you say which you mean.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 2, 'Fourier''s law for a vertical column can be rearranged to give the geothermal gradient. Which expression is it?', '["The thermal conductivity divided by the heat flow, which is k over Q","The heat flow divided by the thermal conductivity, Q over k","Heat flow multiplied by thermal conductivity","Surface temperature multiplied by depth"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The gradient is Q over k. Heat flow is imposed from below and the rock establishes whatever temperature gradient is needed to carry it, so a better conductor develops a gentler slope.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 3, 'In a steady state column with no internal heat production, how does heat flow vary with depth?', '["It falls toward the surface, because some of the heat is absorbed by the rock on the way up","It rises toward the surface as conductivity falls","It varies with the conductivity of each layer","It is the same at every depth"]'::jsonb, 3, 'With no storage term and nothing generating heat inside the column, whatever enters the base has nowhere to go except upward and out, so the same heat flow crosses every level.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 4, 'Using the golden fixture, what is the temperature at the cell centred at 50 m?', '["10 degC, which is the surface value","11.666666666666671 degC","15.000000000000012 degC","41.66666666666673 degC"]'::jsonb, 1, 'T = 10 + 0.06 x 50 / 1.8 gives 11.666666666666671 degC, one of the graded values. 15.000000000000012 degC is the cell at 150 m and 41.66666666666673 degC is the cell at 950 m.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 5, 'What is the temperature at 950 m, the deepest cell of the upper layer?', '["43.333333333333336 degC, which is the value at the layer boundary","38.33333333333339 degC, which is the cell above it","41.66666666666673 degC","59.619047619047684 degC"]'::jsonb, 2, 'T = 10 + 0.06 x 950 / 1.8 gives 41.66666666666673 degC. The boundary value of 43.333333333333336 degC belongs to 1000 m rather than to the cell centre at 950 m.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 6, 'The temperature at the 1000 m layer boundary is 43.333333333333336 degC. Which calculation produces it?', '["10 plus 0.06 times 1000 divided by 1.8, using the upper layer''s conductivity","10 plus 0.06 times 1000 divided by 3.5","The average of the cells at 950 m and 1050 m","The gradient of 17.142857142857142 degC per km applied over 1000 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The boundary is reached from the surface through the upper layer, so it uses that layer''s conductivity of 1.8 W/m/K. The lower layer''s conductivity governs only what happens below the boundary.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 7, 'What is the temperature at the deepest cell of the golden column, centred at 1950 m?', '["75 degC, obtained by continuing the shallow gradient down to that depth","43.333333333333336 degC, the value at the layer boundary","57.90476190476197 degC","59.619047619047684 degC"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Built from the boundary, 43.333333333333336 + 0.06 x 950 / 3.5 gives 59.619047619047684 degC. The 75 degC option is what the extrapolated shallow gradient would have predicted.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 8, 'With a heat flow of 60 mW/m2, what is the gradient in the upper layer where k = 1.8 W/m/K?', '["1.7142857142857142 degC per km","33.333333333333336 degC per km","3.333333333333333 degC per km, which is the rise per 100 m of section","60 degC per km"]'::jsonb, 1, '0.06 W/m2 divided by 1.8 W/m/K gives 33.333333333333336 degC per km. Quoting 3.333333333333333 degC is the per 100 m step for that layer rather than the gradient per kilometre.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 9, 'The upper layer has a gradient of 33.333333333333336 degC per km and the lower layer 17.142857142857142 degC per km. What differs between them?', '["Only the thermal conductivity, since the same 60 mW/m2 passes through both","The heat flow, which drops to roughly half its value on entering the lower layer","Both the heat flow and the thermal conductivity","The surface temperature assumed for each layer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Conservation of energy fixes the heat flow at 60 mW/m2 throughout the steady column, so the whole difference in gradient comes from conductivity, 1.8 W/m/K against 3.5 W/m/K.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 10, 'Which statement about the geothermal gradient is correct?', '["It is a property of the basin, set by the crust beneath it and roughly constant within it","It is a property of the rock, fixed by lithology alone","It is the ratio of heat flow to conductivity at the point where it was measured","It is a property of the earth as a whole"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gradient is Q over k, so it inherits the identity of the rock it was measured in. Treating it as a property of the basin is the misconception this module exists to remove.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 11, 'The 33.333333333333336 degC per km gradient of the upper layer is carried down to 1950 m. What does that give, and how does it compare with the truth?', '["Exactly 75 degC, which overpredicts the true 59.61904761904762 degC by 15.38095238095238 degC","59.61904761904762 degC, which matches the true value","43.333333333333336 degC, which underpredicts the true value","75 degC, which underpredicts the true value"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Extrapolating the shallow gradient gives exactly 75 degC against a true 59.61904761904762 degC, an overprediction of 15.38095238095238 degC. No measurement was wrong, and the whole error comes from assuming the gradient belonged to the earth rather than to the upper layer.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 12, 'You fit a gradient in a thick shale and extrapolate it into a carbonate below it. In which direction is the predicted temperature wrong?', '["Too low, because carbonate conducts heat better and therefore needs a steeper gradient across it","It cannot be said without knowing the heat flow in the carbonate","Neither, provided the heat flow is the same in both","Too high, because the better conductor develops a gentler gradient"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Extrapolating from a low conductivity rock into a high conductivity one overpredicts. The same heat flow crosses both, so the better conductor needs a smaller temperature difference per kilometre.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 13, 'At the 1000 m contact between the two layers of the fixture, what is discontinuous?', '["The gradient, while the temperature itself stays continuous at 43.333333333333336 degC","The temperature, which jumps as the conductivity changes","The heat flow, which steps down on entering the better conductor","Nothing, because both layers carry the same gradient"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rock on either side of a contact is in thermal contact, so temperature cannot jump across a surface of zero thickness. The kink at the boundary is in the slope, which changes from 33.333333333333336 to 17.142857142857142 degC per km.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 14, 'Expressed per 100 m cell, how much does each step add in the lower layer where k = 3.5 W/m/K?', '["3.333333333333333 degC, the same as in the upper layer","17.142857142857142 degC","1.7142857142857142 degC","60 degC"]'::jsonb, 2, '17.142857142857142 degC per km is 1.7142857142857142 degC per 100 m cell, a little over half the 3.333333333333333 degC step of the upper layer.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-heat-in-the-subsurface', 15, 'The lower layer''s conductivity is changed to a value below 3.5 W/m/K and everything else is left alone. What happens at the cell centred at 450 m?', '["It warms, because the column as a whole has become a poorer conductor","It cools, because less heat now reaches the shallow section","It warms or cools depending on the surface temperature","It is unchanged at 25.000000000000032 degC"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Heat flow is fixed at the base and passes upward unchanged, so a conductivity change alters temperatures inside and below that layer while leaving every temperature above it exactly where it was.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 1, 'In this tier, what do decompaction and the steady heat column each contribute to a layer''s temperature history?', '["Decompaction gives the temperature the layer had at each stage of burial, and the heat column supplies the sequence of depths the layer passed through on its way down.","Decompaction supplies the dates of burial, and the heat column converts them into a heating rate.","Decompaction gives the sequence of depths the layer occupied, and the heat column gives the temperature at each of those depths.","Both produce the same information, so one of them is enough."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two halves are geometry and heat. Decompaction produces the depth track and the column produces a temperature at a depth, and only together do they give a temperature at each stage of burial. Neither carries dates, so the depth track has no schedule attached to it at this tier.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 2, 'A 100 m shale whose top sits at 1000 m holds 63.11728183077296 m of grain and restores to 159.79553483785466 m at the surface. Taking the solid thickness of that restored layer at the surface returns 63.117281830772924 m. What does this show?', '["That decompaction moves thickness around and conserves grain, since the two grain figures agree to within 1e-13.","That the restoration grew the grain along with the thickness.","That restoration is only approximate, so the grain figure drifts by roughly a metre every time the layer is restored and buried again.","That the layer was 100 m thick when it was deposited."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Grain is the invariant of decompaction, and the round trip is an identity rather than a trend. The residual of 1e-13 is arithmetic in the last digits, so a restoration that fails to return its own grain is wrong.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 3, 'A restoration returns a thickness smaller than the layer''s present thickness. What has gone wrong?', '["Nothing has gone wrong, because a layer restored from great depth can legitimately come back thinner than it measures today.","The grain density was taken from the wrong lithology in the library, which scales every restored thickness downward.","The porosity was entered as a fraction rather than a percentage.","The two depths were supplied in the wrong order, so the layer was buried rather than restored."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Decompaction adds pore space back, so a restored layer is always thicker than the layer measured today, and the growth increases with burial depth from 134.010303 m at 500 m to 194.513330 m at 2000 m. A restored thickness below the present thickness means the routine was asked to bury rather than restore.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 4, 'Why can you not quote a temperature at 2000 m from the golden heat column?', '["Because the temperature at 2000 m would fall outside the range over which the steady conduction solution is valid.","Because the deepest cell centre is 1950 m, so the column reports nothing at 2000 m.","Because 2000 m sits in the second layer, where the fixture reports gradients in degC per km rather than temperatures.","Because porosity rather than temperature is what the fixture reports below the conductivity break at 1000 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The column is built from ten 100 m cells per layer, so its readings sit at cell centres from 50 m to 1950 m, where the deepest value is 59.619047619047684 degC. Shale porosity at 2000 m is available at 0.22717481230903933 v/v because the compaction curve is continuous, and quoting a temperature there means reading a depth the model does not report.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 5, 'At 1000 m in this fixture, what are the shale porosity and the temperature?', '["0.37831221465172754 v/v and 43.333333333333336 degC, the layer boundary value.","0.22717481230903933 v/v and 43.333333333333336 degC.","0.37831221465172754 v/v and 41.66666666666673 degC.","0.63 v/v and 10 degC."]'::jsonb, 0, '1000 m is one of the two depths where both halves of the fixture report a value. The 0.22717481230903933 v/v belongs at 2000 m and the 41.66666666666673 degC belongs at 950 m, so both distractors are correct values quoted at the wrong depth.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 6, 'The 100 m shale from 1000 m has lost 59.79553483785466 m of thickness to compaction, which is 37.42 percent. A share of what?', '["Of the difference between the restored thickness and the layer''s solid thickness of 63.11728183077296 m.","Of the present thickness of 100 m, which is the thickness the layer measures in the well today.","Of the restored thickness of 159.79553483785466 m, the layer''s original thickness.","Of the layer''s grain thickness of 63.11728183077296 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The percentage is a share of the original thickness the layer had at the surface, which is the restored 159.79553483785466 m. Quoting a compaction loss without its denominator is the same reporting error the Earth Modeling course flagged for mean thickness.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 7, 'Two source rocks both sit at 41.66666666666673 degC at 950 m today. One has been near that depth for most of the basin''s life and the other arrived recently. What can you say from this tier''s tools?', '["That the two rocks are in the same state, since a thermometer reads the same value in both.","That both carry the same present depth and temperature, and that one has been near that temperature far longer, but not which of them is more mature.","That the recent arrival is the more mature, because it was buried faster.","That the long resident has certainly entered the oil window."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Temperature sets the reaction rate and time sets how much of that rate is collected, so equal present temperatures do not mean equal states. Deciding which rock is more mature is a kinetic calculation this tier does not run.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 8, 'Two rocks are heated to 150 degC, one on a 3 degC per Ma ramp and one on a 1 degC per Ma ramp. Compared at that same temperature, which is more mature?', '["The 3 degC per Ma rock, because a faster heating rate drives the reaction harder at every step.","Neither, because maturity is fixed by the highest temperature a rock has reached.","It cannot be decided, because the heating rate has no bearing on how far a reaction has run.","The 1 degC per Ma rock, because it took three times as long to arrive."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Time is a reagent, so the slower ramp leaves the rock more mature even though the comparison is made at equal temperature. The Professional tier turns that statement into numbers, and this tier teaches only the intuition behind it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 9, 'A burial history is built without decompaction, so every old layer is placed at the depth its present thicknesses imply. What does that error become downstream?', '["The old layers are placed too shallow and therefore too cool, so maturity is understated.","The old layers are placed too deep and therefore too hot, so the model overstates maturity at every source rock horizon.","Nothing, because a maturity model reads only the present day depth.","The temperatures are unaffected, since the column ignores thickness."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Compaction has already removed part of every layer above the source rock, so stacking present thicknesses puts the old positions too shallow. A shallower position is a cooler one, and an integrator faithfully converts that geometry error into too little maturity.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 10, 'This tier does not run the clock. What would adding one change?', '["The porosities would stop falling exponentially with depth and would follow the burial rate instead.","The depths become a track against time, with a burial rate for each interval.","The restored thickness of the layer from 1000 m would change from 159.79553483785466 m to a larger value.","The heat column would gain a second conductivity break."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Restoring a layer is a geometric statement about the same grain at a different depth and carries no dates, durations or rates. A clock supplies the schedule that a maturity calculation integrates along, and it changes none of the geometry itself.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 11, 'What would generation and expulsion add that a maturity on its own does not give?', '["A vitrinite reflectance for the source rock at present day.","A temperature history for the source rock through its burial.","A mass of hydrocarbon made and a mass released, which need the source rock''s richness, its hydrogen content and a rule for when it stops holding what it made.","A correction to the compaction curve for organic rich shale."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A transformation ratio is a fraction reacted and says nothing about kilograms, and a generated mass says nothing about how much left the source rock. Both quantities need inputs this tier never collects, which is why charge belongs to the Expert tier.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 12, 'What does it mean that this tier''s heat column is a steady state solution?', '["That heat in equals heat out at every depth, so T = Ts + Qz/k holds exactly.","That the column was solved once at present day and then held fixed while the layers were buried through time.","That the temperature at each depth was averaged over the whole of the basin''s history.","That the surface temperature and the basal heat flow were measured in the well rather than assumed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Steady state means the column has settled, with nothing stored and no internal heat production, which is why 41.66666666666673 degC at 950 m is hand arithmetic. It is a real answer to a narrower question than a thermal history answers.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 13, 'When is the steady state assumption most misleading?', '["When the basin has been quiet for a long time and its basal heat flow has held constant.","When the conductivity contrast between two adjacent layers is large, as it is at the 1000 m break in this fixture.","When the surface temperature is well known.","During rapid burial or an erosion event, when the rock is furthest from settled."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A steady solver has nothing to say about a column still adjusting to cover being added or stripped away. That is why the Expert tier''s 600 m erosion event at 10 Ma and its cooling 80 to 60 mW/m2 heat flow mean nothing to a steady solution.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 14, 'A colleague asks you to read the source rock''s transformation ratio off the burial and heat panel. What is the correct response?', '["Read it from the deepest cell of the column, since transformation ratio rises with temperature and that cell is the hottest in the fixture.","The panel reads compaction, thicknesses and the steady heat column, and produces no maturity.","Set the lithology to shale first, since only source rocks report it.","Convert the porosity at that depth using the grain density."]'::jsonb, 1, 'No kinetic scheme is evaluated anywhere in this tier, so no reflectance and no transformation ratio exists to be read. Expecting the panel to produce a maturity is the classic overreach the scope lesson exists to prevent.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-burial-and-heat-together', 15, 'In the golden column the gradient is 33.333333333333336 degC per km above 1000 m and 17.142857142857142 degC per km below it. What does a gradient that shifts in the middle of a layer of constant conductivity tell you?', '["That the basal heat flow of 60 mW/m2 must be changing with depth inside that layer rather than passing through it unchanged.","That the cell spacing of 100 m is too coarse to resolve the temperature profile inside that layer accurately.","That the conductivity is not what the model was told, or heat is entering or leaving.","Nothing at all, because a geothermal gradient varies continuously within any layer of rock."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The gradient is the ratio Q over k, so it is constant inside a layer of constant conductivity and can only step where k steps. This column has one conductivity break, at 1000 m, and one break in the gradient, with the same 60 mW/m2 flowing through both layers.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 1, 'Which step of the eight step workflow produces no number of its own and yet controls every number after it?', '["Step 6, building the heat column, because the surface temperature and the basal heat flow are chosen rather than measured.","Step 1, assigning the lithology, because phi0 and c set every porosity and thickness in the chain.","Step 5, restoring the layer, because the restored thickness is used by every later calculation in the workflow.","Step 8, handing the result on."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The compaction curve is a property of the lithology, so choosing shale rather than sandstone changes every porosity, every solid thickness and every restored thickness that follows. Assign it from the log rather than from the depth.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 2, 'At 2000 m in the engine''s library, which of shale and sandstone holds the higher porosity, and why?', '["Shale, at 0.63, since it is the most porous of the four lithologies.","Limestone, since it sits between the two at every depth.","They are equal at 2000 m.","Sandstone, at 0.285547 against shale''s 0.227175, because its compaction constant is smaller so it loses porosity more slowly."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Shale is the most porous of the four at the surface, at 0.63 against sandstone''s 0.49, and the order has reversed by 2000 m. The most porous rock at the surface is not the most porous rock at depth, which is why lithology has to be assigned from the log.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 3, 'What does the Beginner tier hand on to the Professional tier?', '["A compaction curve, porosities at depth, solid and restored thicknesses and a steady temperature column, every value with its depth.","A vitrinite reflectance and a transformation ratio for the source rock, which the Professional tier converts into generated and expelled mass.","A single geothermal gradient for the whole basin, quoted in degC per km.","The present day depth of the source rock."]'::jsonb, 0, 'This tier produces the inputs a maturity model consumes and stops there. Handing on a reflectance would mean the tier had run kinetics, and handing on one gradient for the whole basin would average across the conductivity break.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 4, 'Where does this course sit in the geoscience path, and what is its prerequisite?', '["It is the first, since a basin history has to be built before any well is interpreted.","It is the sixth, sitting between Mapping and Rock Physics in the geoscience path.","It is the tenth and last, and its prerequisite is Well Data Manager.","It sits outside the geoscience path, because basin modelling belongs to the reservoir module."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nine earlier courses describe the subsurface as it is today, and this one asks how it got that way, which is why it closes the ladder. A depth reference settled in Well Data Manager is what makes 1000 m mean the same thing in every well.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 5, 'A reviewer opens a burial result and asks which compaction parameters were used. What should the report state?', '["The measured geothermal gradient of the well, since the compaction curve is fitted to it before any porosity is computed.","The lithology with its phi0 of 0.63, its c of 0.00051 per m and its grain density of 2720 kg/m3.","The present day thickness of every layer above the one being restored, since compaction is cumulative down the column.","The number of cells used in the heat column."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A porosity at depth is meaningless without the phi0 and the c behind it, because a different lithology gives a different answer at the same depth. Expect the assignment to have come from a log rather than from an assumption about depth.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 6, 'A reported porosity reads above 1. What has happened, and what would a value above phi0 but below 1 mean instead?', '["A value above 1 is a percentage sitting in a fraction''s place, and a value above phi0 but below 1 points to the wrong lithology or a sign error on depth.","Both cases mean the compaction constant c was entered per km rather than per m.","Both are normal near the surface, where porosity is allowed to exceed phi0.","A value above 1 means the grain density was wrong."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Porosity is a fraction in v/v everywhere in this workflow, bounded below by zero and above by phi0, so both readings are impossible on the Sclater-Christie curve. Entering a percentage where a fraction is wanted is the most common of the two.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 7, 'Which single piece of arithmetic lets a reviewer confirm that the solver was given the inputs the report claims?', '["Multiplying the porosity at 2000 m by the grain density of 2720 kg/m3 to recover the solid thickness of the interval.","Dividing the deepest temperature by its depth to get one gradient.","Adding the two gradients together and halving them.","10 plus 0.06 times 950 divided by 1.8, which gives the 41.66666666666673 degC at 950 m."]'::jsonb, 3, 'In steady state with no internal heat production the column is exactly T = Ts + Qz/k layer by layer, so one value can be reproduced with a calculator. A reviewer who can do that has established that the inputs match the report.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 8, 'Why is a single geothermal gradient quoted for the whole golden column a reporting failure?', '["It is the only defensible way to report a column that contains more than one lithology.","It understates the heat flow, which has to be reported as 0.06 W/m2 instead.","It averages across the conductivity break at 1000 m.","It is acceptable as long as the surface temperature of 10 degC is quoted beside it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The column carries 33.333333333333336 degC per km above the break and 17.142857142857142 degC per km below it, so one averaged figure describes neither layer. Somebody will then extrapolate it into the wrong formation.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 9, 'Besides the numbers themselves, what must a burial and heat report state before anyone builds a maturity model on it?', '["Every value with its depth and unit, that the column is steady state, and that no maturity was computed.","The reflectance of the source rock, so that the reader can judge whether the basin has generated hydrocarbons.","The number of iterations the solver took to converge and the residual it stopped at, so the reader can judge numerical quality.","The name of the software that produced it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A result handed on without the scope statement will have a maturity read into it by the next person who picks it up, and a temperature without its depth will be reused at the wrong depth. Reporting a reflectance would mean the tier had run kinetics it does not run.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 10, 'The capstone grades shale porosity at 2000 m in a field labelled v/v with a tolerance of 0.001. What should you enter?', '["Either form, since the grader accepts a fraction or a percentage for porosity.","0.22717481230903933, because the field is graded in v/v to 0.001 and the percentage form misses by a factor of a hundred.","The percentage form, because porosity is conventionally reported that way on logs.","0.63, the surface value of the curve."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tolerance of 0.001 is one thousandth of a fraction, not one thousandth of a percent. The percentage form of the same porosity is a hundred times larger, so it is not a near miss and it is not partially credited.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 11, 'Which value does the capstone field called 100 m shale from 1000 m, restored to surface want?', '["63.11728183077296 m, the solid thickness the layer has in place at 1000 m, since grain is what the restoration conserves.","100 m, the thickness the layer measures today.","159.79553483785466 m, the restored thickness of the layer whose top is at 1000 m.","38.57953418711555 m, the grain in 100 m at the surface."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field is graded in m to 0.05 and wants the restored thickness, not the grain. Handing in the in place solid thickness of 63.11728183077296 m is the classic loss on this field, and it misses by far more than the tolerance allows.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 12, 'One candidate enters 134.010303 m for the restored thickness field and another enters 194.513330 m. How do those score?', '["The first passes, since 134.010303 m is within 0.05 of the expected value.","Both pass, because the grader accepts a restoration from any depth in the fixture.","The second passes, because a deeper restoration is the safer answer.","Both miss by far more than the 0.05 tolerance."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Those are the restored thicknesses of 100 m of shale found at 500 m and at 2000 m, so both are right answers to a question the field did not ask. The field names its burial depth, and the expected value is 159.79553483785466 m.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 13, 'What does the capstone field called Temperature at the low-k base want?', '["43.333333333333336 degC, the value at the 1000 m layer boundary, which is where the low conductivity layer ends.","41.66666666666673 degC, the centre of the deepest cell in the conductivity 1.8 layer.","44.190476190476254 degC, the first cell below the break, since the base of a layer is read from beneath it.","59.619047619047684 degC, the deepest cell."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The field is read at 950 m and is graded in degC to 0.05. The 1000 m boundary value of 43.333333333333336 degC is the classic near miss here, and it sits far outside the tolerance.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 14, 'What does the capstone field called Temperature at the first cell want, and why is the surface value not the answer?', '["11.666666666666671 degC at 50 m, because the surface value of 10 degC is a boundary condition rather than a graded cell reading.","10 degC, the surface temperature the column starts from.","15.000000000000012 degC at 150 m, the first full cell of the column.","Either value, since both lie within the 0.05 tolerance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The column is built from 100 m cells, so its shallowest reading sits at a cell centre of 50 m. The 10 degC surface node is an input to the solution rather than an output of it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-basin-workflow', 15, 'What does the Professional tier add to the burial and heat result this tier produces?', '["It runs the full four layer reference basin forward over 150 Ma with a cooling 80 to 60 mW/m2 heat flow and a 600 m erosion event at 10 Ma.","It repeats this tier''s workflow on field data rather than on fixtures.","It runs the Sweeney-Burnham Easy%Ro integrator and the Type II kerogen clock over a temperature history.","It replaces the steady column with a measured temperature log."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The Professional tier puts a clock into the burial and heat result and reads maturity from it. The full forward model with its erosion event belongs to the Expert tier, one step further up.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Why can a past depth not be recovered by taking today''s depth and subtracting whatever has been deposited since?', '["Because every layer in between was thicker then, so the section above the horizon has itself been compacted since.","Because sea level has changed, so all depths measured from the present datum carry an unknown offset that varies through time.","Because layers are never deposited horizontally.","Because seismic velocities are uncertain."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A 100 m shale at 1000 m stood at 159.79553483785466 m when its grain sat at the surface, so the overlying section has lost thickness that a simple subtraction ignores. Past depth has to be reconstructed layer by layer.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Which ordering has to hold for a discovery to exist?', '["Charge must arrive before the trap forms, so that the structure grows around fluid that is already in place.","Trap and seal in place before the charge arrives.","The seal must form after the trap has finished filling with hydrocarbons.","Maturity must be reached before the source rock is deposited."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The trap and its seal have to exist before the source expels, or the charge passes through and is lost. That requirement is the timing element, and it is the one that has no physical existence of its own.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'Which statement about what a basin model does not do is correct?', '["It is not a description of the petroleum system elements, since it deals only with heat and never with rock properties.","It is not a volumetric estimate, because counting the barrels inside a mapped structure is what the volumetrics workflow does.","It is not able to date any event in a basin''s history.","It is not built on any assumptions."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Counting barrels in a container is the volumetrics workflow, while a basin model produces a dated history of charge. Dating events is exactly what it does do, and every basin model rests on assumptions about heat flow, lithology and eroded section.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'A learner argues that because the engine and the independent Python oracle agree to about 1e-9, the compaction parameters must be right for the field she is working. What is wrong with that?', '["Nothing is wrong, since the oracle was validated against field data from several basins.","The agreement covers the geometry only and says nothing about porosity.","The agreement is about the code evaluating the written equations, while the parameters are fitted values she has to calibrate.","The oracle uses different equations from the engine."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Agreement between two independent implementations tests the code, not the physics and not the input parameters. Values such as 0.63 and 0.00051 per m for shale are library defaults that a real study calibrates.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'In the golden heat fixture, what is the surface temperature?', '["It is not specified, because a steady state column needs only the basal heat flow and the conductivities.","0.06 degC, the same figure as the basal heat flow.","10 degC, held fixed at the top of the column.","It rises through the run from 10 degC to 60 degC."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fixture fixes 10 degC at the surface and drives 0.06 W/m2 through the column from below. The surface value is needed because the profile is the surface temperature plus the rise accumulated down the column.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'Which of these is a graded capstone value quoted with its correct tolerance?', '["Solid thickness in 100 m of surface shale of 38.57953418711555 m, to a tolerance of 0.05.","The restored thickness of 159.79553483785466 m, to a tolerance of 1e-4 m, which is the agreement between the two implementations.","Shale porosity at 2000 m of 0.22717481230903933 percent, to a tolerance of 0.001.","The grain density of shale of 2720 kg/m3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Thicknesses are graded to 0.05 m and the porosity to 0.001 v/v, and grain density is an input rather than a graded answer. The 1e-4 m figure is how closely the two implementations agree on geometry, which is not a grading tolerance.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'A colleague reports a shale porosity of 0.227 v/v with no depth attached. What is wrong with that?', '["Nothing is wrong, because 0.227 v/v sits inside the graded tolerance of 0.001 and therefore identifies the depth it came from.","The value should have been given as a percentage.","The value is below the surface porosity of 0.63.","A porosity is only meaningful with its depth, since the curve returns a different value at every depth."]'::jsonb, 3, '0.227 v/v is a fact about shale at 2000 m and about no other depth, so a porosity without its depth states nothing. A tolerance says how close an answer has to be, and it cannot supply the missing depth.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'Increase $c$ and hold $\phi_0$ fixed. What happens to the compaction curve?', '["The curve still starts at $\\phi_0$ and bends down harder below it.","The whole curve shifts down by the same proportion at every depth, including at the surface.","The surface porosity falls and the deep porosity is left unchanged.","Nothing changes until the rock reaches 1000 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every curve returns $\phi_0$ at $z = 0$ whatever the constant is, so raising $c$ pivots the curve at the surface and steepens it below. Shifting the whole curve is what a change in $\phi_0$ does, which is the confusion being tested.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'At 3000 m, which of the four lithologies is the most porous and which the least?', '["Sandstone is the most porous at 0.217980 v/v and shale the least at 0.136417 v/v.","Shale is the most porous at 0.136417 v/v and sandstone the least at 0.217980 v/v, keeping the order they had at the surface.","Limestone is the most porous at 0.157472 v/v and dolomite the least at 0.150597 v/v.","All four are equal by 3000 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Sandstone has the smallest compaction constant in the library at 0.00027 per m and shale the largest at 0.00051 per m, so by 3000 m the surface order has been turned over. The first option keeps the surface ranking, which is the misconception.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'Why does a basin model carry solid thickness rather than the thickness read on a log?', '["Because logs measure thickness poorly in shales, so solid thickness is the more accurate of the two measurements.","Because solid thickness is easier to compute than bulk thickness.","Because the grain in a layer does not change with burial, while its bulk thickness is a snapshot valid only at the depth where it was measured.","Because bulk thickness is undefined below 2000 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Compaction moves water out and leaves the mineral where it is, so solid thickness is a property of the layer while bulk thickness is a snapshot valid only at the depth of measurement. Log quality has nothing to do with it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'Shale porosity at 2000 m is graded to a tolerance of 0.001 v/v. How should you handle the digits?', '["Round the stored grid value to three decimal places so that every later step is consistent with the report.","Report all seventeen digits, since that is what the engine returns.","Report 0.227 v/v to the reader and leave the full precision value in the workflow, so that comparisons carry a tolerance instead.","Convert to percent before making any comparison."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Round for the reader, because the fitted parameters do not support more digits, and leave the stored value alone so that comparisons carry a tolerance instead. Rounding inside a workflow is how a small error becomes permanent.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'A 100 m shale found at 1000 m restores to 159.79553483785466 m, so it has lost 59.795535 m of thickness. As a share of its ORIGINAL thickness, how much has it lost?', '["59.795535 percent, taking the loss as a share of the present 100 m.","48.5896 percent, which is the value for a layer found at 2000 m.","25.3789 percent, which is the value for a layer found at 500 m.","37.4200 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The loss of 59.795535 m is taken against the restored 159.79553483785466 m, which gives 37.4200 percent. Dividing by the present 100 m instead is the denominator error, and the other two figures belong to layers found at 500 m and 2000 m.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'A report says a shale layer has lost 37.4200 percent of its thickness to compaction. What has to accompany that number?', '["The grain density of the lithology, since the loss is computed from mass rather than from thickness.","The temperature at the base of the layer at the time it was deposited.","The tolerance the value is graded to.","Which thickness the share is taken of, the original or the present."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A share is meaningless without its denominator, and this one is a share of the original restored thickness rather than of the present 100 m. Stating the denominator is the same discipline the Earth Modeling course insists on for a mean thickness.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'How do you get the mass of mineral standing over each square metre of a layer?', '["Multiply the present thickness by the grain density, since the pore water contributes nothing to the load on the section below.","Multiply the solid thickness by the grain density of its lithology.","Multiply the porosity at the top of the layer by 2720 kg/m3.","Divide the solid thickness by the compaction constant."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Solid thickness times grain density gives the mineral mass per square metre, and it is unaffected by how far the layer has compacted. Using the present thickness counts the pore volume as if it were grain.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'What makes solid thickness usable as the anchor for a restoration?', '["It can be measured directly on a log","Burial changes how far apart the grains sit and never changes how many of them there are","It is the same for every lithology at a given depth","It can be computed as the present thickness multiplied by the average porosity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Solid thickness is a count of how much rock is present, so squeezing water out of a layer leaves it untouched. That invariance is what the restoration is posed around.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'A 100 m shale found at 2000 m holds 77.852091 m of grain while a 100 m shale found at 1000 m holds 63.117282 m. Why?', '["The deeper shale has a higher grain density","The deeper shale was deposited with less pore space in it than the shallower one","Porosity falls with depth, so the deeper interval carries more grain per metre","The deeper shale has been buried for longer, and compaction accumulates with time"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both layers started on the same curve at porosity 0.63. The one at 2000 m has had more of its pore space closed, so the same logged thickness contains more rock.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'Restored thickness grows less per kilometre at depth than it does near the surface. What does that reflect?', '["The porosity curve flattens, so most compaction happens in the first kilometre or so of burial","Grain density rising with depth","Heat flow falling with depth","Porosity reaching zero at about 2000 m, below which no further compaction of the layer is possible"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Going from 500 m to 1000 m adds more restored thickness than going from 2000 m to 3000 m, which is twice the depth interval. Layers that are already deep move very little as they go deeper.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'A decompaction round trip returns the input grain to within about 1e-13 m. What does that indicate?', '["That the compaction curve used is the right one for the rock in this well","That the layer has never been buried more deeply than it is now","That the porosity was measured rather than modelled","That the iteration converged and the step is arithmetically sound"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A mismatch of that size is convergence noise. It confirms the arithmetic and says nothing about whether the curve parameters or the burial assumption are right.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Which number belongs in the project record next to a restored geometry?', '["The number of iterations the solver needed at each layer of the stack","The largest grain mismatch seen anywhere in the run, in metres","The surface porosity and compaction constant of every lithology present","The basal heat flow used"]'::jsonb, 1, 'The largest grain mismatch is the standing quality control number for a restoration. On a healthy run it sits near 1e-13 m and is the least interesting line in the report.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'A restoration produces a set of past columns. Which of these does it not supply?', '["The thickness of each layer in each column","The depth of each layer in each column","The age of each column","The porosity of each layer in each column"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Decompaction is geometry. Ages come from biostratigraphy and from the age model supplied to the study, and the restoration says nothing about when the basin was in each configuration.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'Why is a burial curve built from present-day thicknesses described as too flat as well as too shallow?', '["Compaction is fastest in the first kilometre, so the layers that were shallow at a past moment are the ones most understated","Because the surface temperature is held constant through the run","Because the deepest layer is plotted at its present depth","Because the age model compresses recent time"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The error is not a constant offset. It varies with where each layer sat at each moment, which distorts the rate of burial and therefore the rate at which the layer heated.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'Why does a more porous rock usually have a lower thermal conductivity?', '["Because porosity increases the distance heat has to travel through the sample","Because pore fluid always carries heat away by convection","Because conductivity is defined per unit of solid volume","Because pore water conducts heat poorly compared with rock matrix"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Water is a poor conductor, so the more of a rock that is water the worse it conducts overall. This is also why conductivity rises as a layer compacts.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'Two wells in one basin receive the same heat flow, and well A logs a steeper gradient than well B. What do you need in order to test the likely explanation?', '["The age of the sediments penetrated by each of the two wells","The lithology and porosity over each logged interval","The surface temperature at both well sites","The depth of the deepest mapped reflector at each of the two locations"]'::jsonb, 1, 'With the same Q in both wells a steeper gradient means a lower k over the interval logged. Confirming that needs the lithology and porosity of those intervals, and a check that both gradients cover comparable depth ranges.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'The exact expression T = Ts + Qz/k assumes a steady state. Where does that assumption break down?', '["In any column that contains more than one layer","Wherever the thermal conductivity exceeds 3.5 W/m/K","In a basin that is burying rapidly","Below 2000 m in any sedimentary basin"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Rock stores heat, and while a basin is burying quickly or cooling after a rift that storage matters. The time dependent solver exists for exactly those cases.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'You are handed a basin temperature model whose only quoted number is a single geothermal gradient. What should you ask for?', '["The number of cells the heat solver used in that column","The surface temperature assumed at each time step of the run","The gradient quoted to a greater number of decimal places","The heat flow, and where the gradient was measured"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A heat flow travels between locations because it belongs to the setting. A gradient is tied to the rock it was measured in, so it is meaningless until its interval and lithology are stated.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'What shape does the golden temperature profile have between the surface and 1950 m?', '["Two straight segments joined at 1000 m, the upper one steeper than the lower one","A smooth curve that flattens continuously with depth","A single straight line through all twenty cells","A straight line with a step in temperature at 1000 m"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Conductivity is constant within each layer, so each layer has a constant gradient and a straight profile. The join at 1000 m is a corner in the slope with no step in the temperature.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'A computed profile shows a jump in temperature across a lithology contact. What does that indicate?', '["A conductivity contrast at the contact large enough to break the continuity of temperature","A bug, most often two segments each computed from the surface rather than chained","Internal heat production in the lower layer","A basal heat flow that was set too high"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Temperature is continuous across a contact whatever the conductivity contrast. A step means the second segment never inherited the base temperature of the first.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'The 15.38095238095238 degC overprediction at 1950 m comes from which mistake?', '["A basal heat flow set too high by nearly a factor of two","Measuring the shallow gradient over too short a depth interval","Assuming a gradient measured in one rock applies in a rock of different conductivity","Rounding the conductivity of the lower layer"]'::jsonb, 2, 'The heat flow of 60 mW/m2 was right and the shallow gradient of 33.333333333333336 degC per km was right for the layer it was measured in. Carrying it into a rock of conductivity 3.5 W/m/K is what produced the error.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'You measure the 33.333333333333336 degC per km gradient in the upper layer and carry it below 1000 m. What does it predict at 1950 m, and what does that cost?', '["59.619047619047684 degC, the value the column already reports.","About 43 degC, since the gradient halves below the break.","It cannot be predicted without the heat flow.","Exactly 75 degC, an overprediction of 15.38095238095238 degC against the true value at that depth."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Extrapolating a gradient measured in one formation into another treats Q over k as a property of the earth. At source rock depth an error of that size is the difference between a rock that never entered the oil window and one well inside it.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'The capstone field called Temperature at the deepest cell asks for a value in degC to 0.05. Which is it?', '["The temperature at the 2000 m base of the column, since that is the deepest point the fixture describes.","59.619047619047684 degC at 1950 m, the deepest cell centre.","43.333333333333336 degC at the 1000 m boundary.","41.66666666666673 degC at 950 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The column is built from 100 m cells, so its deepest reading is a cell centre at 1950 m rather than the 2000 m base. Reading a temperature at the base of the column is a depth the fixture does not report.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'The capstone field called Solid thickness in 100 m of surface shale is graded in m to 0.05. Which value does it want?', '["63.11728183077296 m, the grain the same 100 m of shale holds once its top has reached 1000 m.","51.655586470092686 m, the grain in 100 m of sandstone at the surface, which is the reference case.","38.57953418711555 m, the grain inside 100 m of freshly deposited shale.","159.79553483785466 m."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The field is the surface case for shale, so 100 m of fresh shale is about 38.6 m of grain and the rest is pore. The buried grain and the sandstone value are both right answers to questions the field did not ask.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'How are the tolerances distributed across the six graded capstone fields?', '["0.001 on the porosity and 0.05 on the other five.","0.05 on all six fields, since the grader uses one window per capstone.","0.001 on all six, because the fixtures are computed to many decimal places.","0.05 on the porosity and 0.001 on the two thickness and three temperature fields."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The porosity field is graded in v/v to 0.001, and the two thickness fields in m and the three temperature fields in degC all carry 0.05. Swapping the two windows would make the porosity field far too loose and the others far too tight.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A candidate enters the porosity at 2000 m in percent rather than as a fraction. How does that field score?', '["It passes, because the grader normalises percentages.","It misses the 0.001 window by a factor of a hundred and scores nothing, since the field is graded in v/v.","It scores partial credit for having the right digits.","It fails only if the sign is also wrong."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The tolerance of 0.001 is one thousandth of a fraction, and the percentage form of the same porosity is a hundred times larger. That is not a near miss and it is not partially credited.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'A reviewer hands you a burial and heat result and asks whether the source rock is in the oil window. What is the correct answer?', '["Read the deepest temperature of 59.619047619047684 degC and compare it with a published oil window range.","Use the porosity at that depth as a proxy for maturity.","Report the gradient instead.","Say that this tier cannot answer it, and hand on the temperature history for a maturity model."]'::jsonb, 3, 'No kinetic scheme is evaluated in this tier, so no maturity exists in the result. Comparing a single temperature against a published window is the overreach the scope lesson exists to prevent, because it ignores how long the rock has been there.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'What would replacing the steady heat column with transient heat give you?', '["A sequence of columns, each starting from the one before with that step''s burial applied.","A single column solved to a higher numerical tolerance, so the temperatures carry more significant figures.","A second conductivity break in the column, so the gradient steps twice between 0 and 2000 m.","Nothing, because the steady answer is exact."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A transient solution lags the steady answer while the basin is burying or eroding, which is when the difference matters most. A cooling heat flow and an erosion event mean nothing to a steady solver.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which statement describes what this tier actually does?', '["It builds burial and heat and then integrates a kinetic scheme to report a reflectance for the source rock.","It runs the basin forward through its history and reports generated and expelled mass in kg/m2.","It builds burial and heat on fixtures in steady state.","It computes a transformation ratio but stops short of generation and expulsion."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tier has no clock, no kinetics and no mass balance, and its heat column is a settled snapshot. Each of the other three describes a job that belongs to the Professional or the Expert tier.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'What does shale porosity do by 4000 m on the Sclater-Christie curve?', '["It reaches zero at 4000 m, where the pore space has closed.","0.08191808785340832 at 4000 m, still positive and still below the surface value of 0.63.","0.13641747040908445 at 4000 m.","It turns negative below about 3500 m."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The curve is exponential, so porosity falls steeply and approaches zero without reaching it. The 0.13641747040908445 belongs at 3000 m, which is a right value quoted at the wrong depth.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'What is the erosion signature that the Expert tier recovers, and how?', '["A delta Ro of 0.0567, the difference between the run with the erosion event and the run without it.","The 600 m of section removed at 10 Ma, read directly from the present day thickness of the layer that was eroded away.","The drop in heat flow from 80 to 60 mW/m2.","The 150 Ma duration of the model."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The tier runs the same reference basin twice and differences the two final reflectances. Recovering a number for something that is no longer there, by running the model twice, is a good picture of what a basin model is for.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'What sequence does the platform enforce before the capstone opens?', '["Module quizzes at 70 percent and the final exam at 75 percent, with the capstone available at any point.","Module quizzes at 75 percent with no retry limit, and a final exam that is optional once the capstone is passed.","There are no thresholds.","Quizzes at 75 percent, a cooldown after three fails, then the exam at 70 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every lesson is read, each module quiz needs 75 percent, three consecutive failures trigger a 24 hour cooldown, and the final exam needs 70 percent. Passing the capstone last is what grants the Associate certification.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'In the eight step workflow, which step comes immediately before restoring the layer?', '["Building the heat column, since the temperature sets how far the layer has compacted.","Reading the porosity at 2000 m from the Sclater-Christie curve for that lithology.","Taking the grain of the buried layer, 63.11728183077296 m.","Converting the solid thickness back into a bulk thickness at the same depth."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Restoration holds the grain constant and asks what thickness it occupies at a shallower depth, so the grain of the layer in place has to be computed first. Heat is a separate build that starts only once the geometry is finished.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'How is the golden two layer heat column discretised?', '["Ten 100 m cells per layer, so the centres run from 50 m to 1950 m, with 10 degC at the surface and 0.06 W/m2 at the base.","Twenty 50 m cells per layer, with centres running from 25 m to 1975 m.","One cell per layer, since the solution is analytic.","Cells at the layer boundaries only, at 0, 1000 and 2000 m."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The cell layout is what puts the graded readings at 50 m, 950 m and 1950 m rather than at round depths. It is also why the column reports nothing at the 2000 m base.'),
  ('basin', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'Which statement about time and temperature is correct?', '["Temperature sets how much reaction is possible and time sets the maximum the rock can reach, so the two are interchangeable.","Temperature sets the rate of the reaction and time sets how much of that rate is collected.","Time sets the rate and temperature sets the ceiling.","Only temperature matters once a threshold has been passed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Maturity is what you get after collecting rate over time, which is why a rock held near a threshold for a long time is not equivalent to one taken past it briefly. The higher tier turns that into a kinetic integral.');
